#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084092} :activity/id #uuid "f0036e42-d6e1-5a92-99f0-f7897f818532" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050217 :topic/id #uuid "3707354f-3234-5238-b260-8dcbc8405db7" :topic/math-academy-id 760} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084388 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're trying to find the **arc length** of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b.$ The arc length of a typical function between two points is shown below:\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nIn this image, the solid line represents the length that we're trying to find, and the dotted line represents the continuation of the curve $y=f(x)$ beyond the points of interest.\n\nThe arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6cef2d6-0611-5e58-b127-47c3b537067e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e30a6e68-0f69-5f3c-9066-8d1faed363f9" :step/math-academy-id 1974 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083008 :question/id #uuid "b293a58f-0903-5ee7-ab4b-3cbc2e5e4b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368568}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"} :step/id #uuid "503684ad-7ed7-5631-b233-b8294b8c6b14" :step/math-academy-id 1975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083009 :question/id #uuid "cf9adf09-b14f-5b5b-a110-60354b93c241" :question/math-academy-id "e-561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440931}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"} :step/id #uuid "1a3b431a-28d4-5ffa-84fe-0570a5b35b3f" :step/math-academy-id 1979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083302 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083102 :question/id #uuid "80794b09-e0f4-5bff-8c0c-8261638f9613" :question/math-academy-id "e-7268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401055}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "83509829-52cc-54fc-aff4-e35b85b466d7" :step/math-academy-id 26917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084473 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nFirst, we divide the curve into small subintervals. Consider a part of the blue arc that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of that part be $\\Delta{s}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b4d9b2e66ae976ac136170f13525240b02f3f10b3887d6b19738088be528ff.png)\n\nWe can now denote the horizontal displacement as $\\Delta{x}$ and the vertical shift as $\\Delta{y}.$ \n\n![](images/7f/7fe992a709634e9be41f8a2c6d12f7bc72c6f9e8105c19f5f5a47e98c3673b06.png)\n\nTo approximate the length $\\Delta{s}$ we apply the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δs\\approx \\sqrt{{(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{Δy}{Δx})}^{2}}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo approximate the total length $L,$ we need to sum up all of the little $\\Delta s$ terms, so\n\n$$\nL \n\\approx \\sum \\Delta s = \\sum \\sqrt{\\left( \\Delta{x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\Delta{y} \\right)^2}\n= \\sum \\left( \\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\Delta{y}}{\\Delta{x}} \\right)^2} \\cdot \\Delta{x} \\right).\n$$\n\nWe then take the limit as $\\Delta x, \\Delta y \\rightarrow 0.$ As we do this, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}s & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nthe sum becomes a definite integral, and our approximation approaches the exact value of $L{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int \\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8eece214-0f4a-5143-bf64-d939687b9276" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4576 :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind the Main Formula"} :step/id #uuid "b91df088-62b4-5fea-901c-a934dc9c4398" :step/math-academy-id 28187}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Planar Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083450}]}
